<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kuroda Nagahiro</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1811">1811</a>–<a href="/years/1887">1887</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Kuroda Nagahiro was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/daimyo">daimyo</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fukuoka">Fukuoka</a> domain in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyushu">Kyushu</a> from 1834 to 1869. Born into the Shimazu family of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/satsuma-domain">Satsuma</a> and adopted by the Kuroda, he promoted Western learning, studying <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> science and encouraging military and industrial improvements connected with the domain's duty of guarding <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nagasaki">Nagasaki</a>. During the 1860s he tried to mediate between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shogun">shogunate</a> and the anti-shogunate domains.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kuroda-nagahiro.jpg" alt="Kuroda Nagahiro" width="320" height="480" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:KurodaNagahiro.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
